WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.370
- We are prepared to make
you a very generous offer.

00:00:02.370 --> 00:00:04.773
- And we are prepared
to reject that offer.

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:06.660
- Michael, you haven't even heard.

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:09.030
- We never accept their first offer.

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:10.470
What is your second offer?

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:13.530
- Stop for a second, it's just
not, makes no sense to say

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:14.460
I don't even hear your first offer,

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:15.293
I wanna hear your second offer.

00:00:15.293 --> 00:00:16.560
I'm Guhan Subramanian.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:18.180
I'm a professor at Harvard Law School

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:20.520
and Harvard Business School
and I'm the faculty chair

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:22.470
of the Harvard Law School
Program on Negotiation.

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:24.960
We're gonna watch a few negotiation clips,

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:27.780
and I'm gonna offer a
negotiation perspective

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:30.060
on some of the dynamics that
we'll see on the screen.

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:31.890
- What is your second offer?

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:33.033
- 12,000 dollars.

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:35.280
- Are you kidding me?

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:37.170
That is insultingly low.

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:39.360
I don't even wanna hear
what your first offer was.

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:41.550
- I think in general you should put

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:43.110
a first offer on the table.

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:45.270
And so especially as a
seller of the business,

00:00:45.270 --> 00:00:46.800
you should try to anchor the negotiation

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:48.090
at a very high number,

00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:49.950
and that's by putting a
first offer out there.

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:53.580
- I'm sure you're
scared, probably in debt.

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:55.590
That's the best offer you're gonna get.

00:00:55.590 --> 00:00:58.620
- I'll see your situation and
I'll raise you a situation.

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:01.860
Your company is losing
clients left and right.

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:04.080
You have a stockholder meeting coming up,

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:05.910
and you're going to
have to explain to them

00:01:05.910 --> 00:01:10.020
why your most profitable
branch is bleeding.

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:12.150
- As much of a buffoon
as Michael Scott is,

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:13.860
this is a really nice move.

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:16.560
So in negotiations
there's some terminology

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:18.120
we oftentimes use.

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:23.120
The BATNA is the best alternative
to a negotiated agreement.

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:26.880
So the BATNA is what will you
do if you don't reach a deal?

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:28.800
And equally important, their BATNA,

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:30.690
what will they do if
they don't reach a deal?

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:33.510
Michael Scott does a really
nice job of saying, look,

00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:35.400
you think my BATNA is so bad?

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:37.290
Let me tell you about your BATNA.

00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:39.060
And your BATNA is you're
gonna lose your job

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:40.320
if you don't get this thing done.

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:44.280
So it's a really nice
display of pretty textbook,

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:45.930
best practice negotiations.

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:49.023
- How's about 60,000 dollars?

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:52.620
- That's a five X increase
from his first offer.

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:55.590
So it does suggest that
the BATNA assessment

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:57.990
of Michael Scott was
pretty correct in that

00:01:57.990 --> 00:02:00.030
he has to reflect that in
the next offer that he makes.

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:01.380
- My client is prepared to settle

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:03.513
for 50 percent of the marital assets.

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:05.640
- Obviously it's a farce.

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:07.380
It's a nice way of trying
to diffuse the offer

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:08.520
that's being made.

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:10.560
- Why only 50 Freddy?

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:11.880
Why not a hundred?

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:13.650
While we're dreaming, why not 150?

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:16.530
- So in negotiations, we've
got a concept called ZOPA,

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:17.850
zone of possible agreement.

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:19.290
When you make a first offer,

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:21.480
you can anchor the
negotiation in your favor.

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:25.110
The research indicates that
it shifts our perception

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:27.420
of where the zone of
possible agreement is.

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:29.700
In this case, you go to the
room and think you can get

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:33.090
50 percent of the marital assets,
but when the anchor is set

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:34.530
at something more aggressive than that,

00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:36.780
you feel like I'll be lucky
if I can get 25 percent.

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:38.730
So, anchors shift our perception.

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:39.690
- What's your bottom line?

00:02:39.690 --> 00:02:41.340
- Primary residence, 30
percent of remaining assets.

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:42.570
- What are you nuts?

00:02:42.570 --> 00:02:46.080
- So in general, repeat
negotiators have to be more

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:47.340
problem solving with each other

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:49.240
because there's gonna be a next round.

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:53.970
- Freddy, it's a negotiation.

00:02:53.970 --> 00:02:57.870
- Walking out of the negotiation
is an appropriate tactic.

00:02:57.870 --> 00:03:00.720
To stay at the table in
this context would signal,

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:03.300
I'll be willing to tolerate your antics,

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:05.970
and you wanna be able to signal,
it's just not acceptable.

00:03:05.970 --> 00:03:07.980
I often get asked, should
I go to their office

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:09.270
or should they come to my office?

00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:11.700
I recommend, and I know it's not easy,

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:13.140
but go to their office.

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:14.730
It shows some respect for their time

00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:16.710
that you're coming to them,
they're not coming to you.

00:03:16.710 --> 00:03:19.230
If things really break
down, you can leave.

00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:21.510
- 65.
- 85 Ari.

00:03:21.510 --> 00:03:22.680
- I'll give you a 75,

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:25.410
provided that after I do my due diligence,

00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:28.350
I don't find out that you
screwed up everything I did here.

00:03:28.350 --> 00:03:29.910
Yes or no?

00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:33.060
- The midpoint of the
first reasonable offer

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:35.790
and counter offer is typically
a pretty good predictor

00:03:35.790 --> 00:03:37.620
where the final deal's gonna end up.

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:40.740
- 85, that's my drop dead
number, not a dollar less.

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:42.270
I don't care what you think you know.

00:03:42.270 --> 00:03:44.280
- I think that's a potential problem.

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:49.230
When you say things like drop
dead, absolute best and final,

00:03:49.230 --> 00:03:51.150
this is really my limit,

00:03:51.150 --> 00:03:54.240
it does reduce your
credibility for the future

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:56.970
if you then agree to
something much less than.

00:03:56.970 --> 00:03:58.560
- I'll need to hire my own team.

00:03:58.560 --> 00:03:59.490
- We can make that work.

00:03:59.490 --> 00:04:00.780
- The other firm said they'd lose

00:04:00.780 --> 00:04:02.190
a billable hours requirement.

00:04:02.190 --> 00:04:03.780
- We just want you working on the cases

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:05.010
that you are passionate about.

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:06.780
- She's playing them off each other,

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:08.370
and sometimes it's very explicit.

00:04:08.370 --> 00:04:12.270
In my research, I do find that
the competition for the deal

00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:14.100
or the perception of competition

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:15.630
can create a lot more leverage

00:04:15.630 --> 00:04:17.280
than just the back and
forth across the table.

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:18.600
And this is an example of that.

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:20.190
- I'll need complete independence.

00:04:20.190 --> 00:04:23.250
No supervisor, no staff
meetings, no backseat driving.

00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:25.170
- You're great because
you're you Annalise.

00:04:25.170 --> 00:04:28.200
- Early on, you wanna
think about the broad set

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:31.020
of features of the job,
your responsibility,

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:32.580
who do you report to?

00:04:32.580 --> 00:04:34.350
What does the trajectory look like?

00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:36.390
Pay is important, for sure.

00:04:36.390 --> 00:04:40.860
As you get more senior,
things like elimination

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:44.580
of administrative tasks, you
know, being able to focus

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:45.543
on what you love.

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:49.200
Less about mentorship
and more about mentoring.

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:50.910
Those things become more
important in the mix.

00:04:50.910 --> 00:04:52.860
- I've been offered double that amount.

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:54.750
- I find that hard to believe.

00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:56.550
- Call to check, I've got offers from

00:04:56.550 --> 00:04:58.800
Lindstrom Shorish Group,
Summers and Powers.

00:04:58.800 --> 00:04:59.790
- Summers and Powers is ranked

00:04:59.790 --> 00:05:01.260
the number one firm in Pennsylvania.

00:05:01.260 --> 00:05:04.320
- If it's a weaker firm,
you might not wanna mention

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:06.930
who it is but you could just
say, I've got another offer.

00:05:06.930 --> 00:05:10.410
If it's a competitor firm
or even a better firm,

00:05:10.410 --> 00:05:12.420
you might wanna put
that name on the table.

00:05:12.420 --> 00:05:14.880
- Give me a few days
and I'll think about it.

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:16.350
Lemme think about it.

00:05:16.350 --> 00:05:17.490
I'll think about it.

00:05:17.490 --> 00:05:19.950
- I think in this context
to say, "Give me a few days

00:05:19.950 --> 00:05:22.650
to think about it," that
is entirely appropriate.

00:05:22.650 --> 00:05:24.420
Consult with your spouse,
consult with your family.

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:25.650
Think about what you wanna do.

00:05:25.650 --> 00:05:27.420
It allows you to shop around some more.

00:05:27.420 --> 00:05:31.380
Now, on the law firm side,
some of the lower contenders

00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:32.610
might have considered making what I call

00:05:32.610 --> 00:05:33.840
a shutdown down move.

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:36.450
When she says, "Give me a
few days to think about it,"

00:05:36.450 --> 00:05:38.730
the law firm might counter and say, gosh,

00:05:38.730 --> 00:05:40.950
is there anything we could do right now

00:05:40.950 --> 00:05:43.470
that would make it easy for
you to accept our offer, right?

00:05:43.470 --> 00:05:45.900
That's the kind of thing you
could try to do to shut it down

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:48.210
and to get it done in the
room because you're aware

00:05:48.210 --> 00:05:49.620
that if she leaves the room,

00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:51.030
she's probably not gonna come back.

00:05:51.030 --> 00:05:53.970
In mergers and acquisitions
and other contexts,

00:05:53.970 --> 00:05:55.980
if you take a few extra days,

00:05:55.980 --> 00:05:57.270
the deal can get taken away from you.

00:05:57.270 --> 00:06:00.870
So in general, you wanna
keep the deal momentum going.

00:06:00.870 --> 00:06:02.753
- Take time to think about it.

00:06:03.690 --> 00:06:05.850
- I don't need to, it's not a good fit.

00:06:05.850 --> 00:06:07.680
- We just said yes to every
one of your asks Ms Keating.

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:09.870
- Well that's because
you're basically desperate.

00:06:09.870 --> 00:06:13.350
- You might consider saying,
hey, I'm about to walk away.

00:06:13.350 --> 00:06:15.270
I think there's just no deal to be done,

00:06:15.270 --> 00:06:17.910
but before I do that,
gimme your best and final.

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:19.800
So to give them a so-called last look

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:21.720
can be appropriate before walking away.

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:24.300
But sure, if there's six
or seven possibilities,

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:26.010
drill at one or two,
they're just non-starters.

00:06:26.010 --> 00:06:27.300
That's entirely appropriate.

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:31.350
The way she's deployed
competition and the perception

00:06:31.350 --> 00:06:33.000
of competition is really pretty terrific.

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:33.833
It's pretty good.

00:06:33.833 --> 00:06:37.140
- I brought in the Popsicle
account today on my own.

00:06:37.140 --> 00:06:39.150
- Hey Ginger, did you hear about this?

00:06:39.150 --> 00:06:39.983
I've gotta go.

00:06:39.983 --> 00:06:41.820
- So here she does a
pretty good job of saying,

00:06:41.820 --> 00:06:44.310
look here's what I do,
here's what I've done.

00:06:44.310 --> 00:06:45.420
Here's what I've delivered.

00:06:45.420 --> 00:06:46.470
And because of that.

00:06:46.470 --> 00:06:49.680
- Freddie Robinson's office
has been vacant for some time.

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:50.930
I think I should have it.

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:54.900
- It's yours.
- Really?

00:06:54.900 --> 00:06:58.620
- The thing that gives
me some concern is that

00:06:58.620 --> 00:07:01.260
she asked for something quite reasonable,

00:07:01.260 --> 00:07:03.390
and I wonder is she under asking?

00:07:03.390 --> 00:07:06.900
Is she under advocating for herself?

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:09.810
My recommendation would be being aware

00:07:09.810 --> 00:07:12.450
of what you're delivering,
and then making an offer

00:07:12.450 --> 00:07:15.060
or asking for something
that's reflecting the value

00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:16.230
you're delivering to the company.

00:07:16.230 --> 00:07:17.940
What's the ZOPA in this negotiation?

00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:19.650
And ask for it.

00:07:19.650 --> 00:07:21.630
I'm a little disappointed,
and maybe this is

00:07:21.630 --> 00:07:24.630
just the inevitable given
the context that she's in.

00:07:24.630 --> 00:07:27.120
- You young women are very aggressive.

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:28.350
- Oh, I didn't mean to be impolite.

00:07:28.350 --> 00:07:29.550
- She's not being aggressive.

00:07:29.550 --> 00:07:30.780
She's asking something very reasonable.

00:07:30.780 --> 00:07:35.370
In the US, there's research
indicating that women

00:07:35.370 --> 00:07:38.550
in general, on average, are less likely

00:07:38.550 --> 00:07:42.510
to perceive situations as
being negotiable than are men.

00:07:42.510 --> 00:07:46.440
And as a result, they
don't perceive situations

00:07:46.440 --> 00:07:48.000
as being opportunities to negotiate.

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:53.000
Now a further troubling finding
is that there is a backlash

00:07:53.220 --> 00:07:54.720
when women negotiate.

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:56.940
And this guy illustrates that
very clearly on the screen

00:07:56.940 --> 00:07:59.130
when he says, you know,
you're kind of a pain,

00:07:59.130 --> 00:08:00.180
you're being very aggressive.

00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:01.770
She asked for something, she got it.

00:08:01.770 --> 00:08:03.840
Hopefully today she
could ask for a lot more.

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:04.690
- How much is it?

00:08:06.270 --> 00:08:09.423
- 8,600.
- We will give you 10 dollars.

00:08:10.920 --> 00:08:14.010
- Now, low balling is
a version of anchoring.

00:08:14.010 --> 00:08:17.610
If it's reasonable, then it
can be an effective anchor.

00:08:17.610 --> 00:08:19.650
If it's ridiculous, it has no effect.

00:08:19.650 --> 00:08:22.080
And in this case, the 10
dollars is clearly ridiculous.

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:23.490
It has no effect.

00:08:23.490 --> 00:08:24.780
- Are you interested in this ring?

00:08:24.780 --> 00:08:28.780
- Yes, yes, but I can
only pay 8,000 dollars.

00:08:28.780 --> 00:08:30.240
- Okay, I can let it go for eight.

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:31.620
- This is a bit rushed.

00:08:31.620 --> 00:08:34.800
And so when the guy says,
well, what's your offer,

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:37.710
to take a little step
away, talk to Phoebe,

00:08:37.710 --> 00:08:40.110
come back and say, well
what do you think of 7,000,

00:08:40.110 --> 00:08:41.790
to me that would've been appropriate.

00:08:41.790 --> 00:08:44.100
To just come up with a number,

00:08:44.100 --> 00:08:45.570
that's when mistakes can be made.

00:08:45.570 --> 00:08:47.430
The only way to really reset

00:08:47.430 --> 00:08:50.220
when that kinda mistake is
made, is to change the players.

00:08:50.220 --> 00:08:53.640
Joey comes back the next day
and offers 7,000 for the ring

00:08:53.640 --> 00:08:55.740
and they get it done at 7,500,

00:08:55.740 --> 00:08:58.110
and then he sells it back
to Chandler for that price.

00:08:58.110 --> 00:09:00.750
Negotiations is a skill you
can really work on and learn

00:09:00.750 --> 00:09:02.640
over the course of a career.

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:04.293
Can you become Roger Federer?

00:09:05.250 --> 00:09:06.870
You know, probably not.

00:09:06.870 --> 00:09:08.640
With all the tennis lessons in the world,

00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:09.750
I can't become Roger Federer.

00:09:09.750 --> 00:09:11.010
But you can get really good,

00:09:11.010 --> 00:09:13.403
and I think that's true
in negotiations as well.

